[0.000, "o", "      \u001b[25;1H\u001b[94m1.\u001b[93m FUN_1400234bc\u001b[1m @ 0x1400234bc = standard base64 decode\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m — builds its reverse-lookup table from the literal alphabet\r\nABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789+/ (standard table, no rotation), 6-bit accumulator loop visible in decompile.\r\n\u001b[94m2.\u001b[93m FUN_140023048\u001b[1m @ 0x140023048 = textbook RC4\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m — 256-byte KSA (S[i]=i, j=j+S[i]+key[i % keylen], swap) followed by PRGA XOR (out[n] =\r\nS[(S[i]+S[j])&0xff] ^ in[n]). Key bytes are read from the\u001b[1m global std::string at\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m DAT_1400b0b30 (key[i % DAT_1400b0b40], length slot 0x1400b0b40).\r\n\u001b[39m \u001b[C                          \r\n\u001b[93mSo the scheme is exactly the suspected Stealc v2 shape:\u001b[C\u001b[1mplaintext = RC4(base64_decode(blob), key)\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m with a single global key for all blobs.\u001b[32;89H\u001b[39m \u001b[36m\u001b[1m⠋ \u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[90mThinking…\u001b[39m \u001b[35m\u001b[1mReverseEngin\u001b[Cer(Ganymede)\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[37m: ca\u001b[Cl\u001b(B\u001b[m"]
