[0.002, "o", "\u001b[93me\u001b[Cembedded PE in .data (file offset 0x890D0, see #q-embedded-pe) sits\r\ninside the 5.36-entropy .data region, i.e. it is stored\u001b[1m unencrypted\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m. The obfuscation in this sample is at string granularity (base64/RC4 strings, see\r\n#q-string-decryption), not section packing. Verdict:\u001b[1m not packed, no encrypted sections\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m.\r\n\u001b[39m                                                                                                                                                \r\n\u001b[97m🤖\u001b[36m\u001b[1m Researcher(Amalthea)\u001b(B\u001b[m                                                                                                                          \r\n\u001b[97mAll eight checklist comments are posted. Now I'll mark the question solved, cross-link the relevant open questions, and flag the recall-based items\u001b[39m  \r\n\u001b[97mfor analyst awareness:\u001b[39m                                                                 \u001b[42;101H\u001b[90m62\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[C\u001b[90m509\u001b[9C2\u001b[3C7\u001b[10C3\u001b[C025\u001b[C256\u001b[4C9\u001b[C1131\u001b[34;2H\u001b(B\u001b[m"]
[0.052, "o", "\u001b[4;1H\u001b[32m●\u001b[C\u001b[90mResearcher(Amalthea) · ▸ set_status(#q-family-attribution) · 15757 chars\r\n\u001b[39m \u001b[C                                                   \r\n\u001b[93m💬 reply #q-string-decryption\u001b[36m\u001b[1m Researcher(Amalthea)\u001b(B\u001b[m                          \r\n\u001b[93m[external knowledge] Expected scheme per Stealc family reporting (details + falsification criteria in !57 under #q-family-attribution):\u001b[1m base64 decode\r\n→ RC4 decrypt\u001b[Cwith a single hardcoded ASCII key\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m (key ~8–32 bytes, stored in\u001b[Ccleartext in the binary).\u001b[CLook for one heavily cross-\u001b[Ceferenced \u001b[Coutine\u001b[39m \r\n\u001b[93mwi\u001b[Ch a classic RC4 KSA/PRGA (256-iter S-box loop\u001b[Cw\u001b[Cth swaps)\u001b[Cfed by a\u001b[Cstandard-alphab\u001b[Ct base64 decoder. The same k\u001b[Cy likely decrypts C2 tr\u001b[Cffic\r\n(relevant to #q-crypto-keys). The decrypted set should include the C2 URL and build ID — Stealc's embedded co\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93mnfig is minimal; target lists come from\r\nthe server.\u001b[39m                                                                                                            \u001b[13;1H\u001b[93m💬 reply #q-embedded-pe\u001b[36m\u001b[1m Researcher(Amalthea)\u001b(B\u001b[m                                     \r\n\u001b[93m[external knowledge] Working hypothesis (full checks in !62 under #q-family-attribution): in the Stealc\u001b[1m v2\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m rewrite, the embedded PE is most plausibly\r\na\u001b[1m Chrome App-Bound Encryption (ABE) bypass helper\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m injected into/run in Chrome's context to recover app-bound cookies/keys (v1 had no embedded PE — it\r\ndownloaded helper DLLs from \u001b[C2).\u001b[CVerify\u001b[Cvia its strings (app_bound,\u001b[Cos_crypt,\u001b[Cencrypted_key, elevation, IElevator CLSID\r\n{708860E0-F641-4611-8895-7D867DD3675B}, named pipes) and\u001b[Cthe\u001b[Chost-side consumer\u001b[C(injection APIs gated on a Chrome process). Alternative to rule out:\r\na\u001b[Cgeneric\u001b[C\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93mdropped next-stage payload written to disk and executed.\u001b[39m               \r\n                                                                                 \r\n\u001b[93m💬 \u001b[2Cply\u001b[C#q-ransomware\u001b[36m\u001b[1m Researcher(Amalthea)\u001b(B\u001b[m                                      \r\n\u001b[93m[external knowledge] Prior from family attribution (#q-family-attribution, YARA-matched Stealc): Stealc has\u001b[1m no ransomware capability\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m in any public\r\nv1/v2 reporting — no file encryption of user data, no ransom notes, no shadow-copy deletion. Its crypto use is RC4 for string/traffic obfuscation and\r\nDPAPI/AES-GCM handling for\u001b[3m decrypting stolen browser credentials\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m, which can superficially trip ransomware heuristics. Falsifiable\u001b[Crefutation checks:\r\nabsence of vssadmin/wmic shadowc\u001b[Cpy/bcdedit strings, no ransom\u001b[Cnot\u001b[2Cliterals,\u001b[Cno mass file-enumer\u001b[Ction +\u001b[Crewri\u001b[Ce-in-place loop; fil\u001b[C enu\u001b(B\u001b[m"]
[0.000, "o", "\u001b[93mmeration\u001b[39m \r\n\u001b[93mpresent should f\u001b[Ced the\u001b[3m grabber/exfil\u001b[C\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93mpath\u001b[C(read\u001b[C+ upload), not encrypt-and-overwrite.\u001b[39m                                                               \r\n                                                                                       \r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[C\u001b[90mResearcher(Amalthea) · ▸ flag_for_review(researcher) · 930 chars\r\n\u001b[39m \u001b[C                     \r\n\u001b[97m🤖\u001b[36m\u001b[1m researcher\u001b(B\u001b[m                                                                                                                                      \r\n\u001b[97m⚑ fl\u001b[Cgged for \u001b[Cnaly\u001b[Ct review: Stealc v2 checklist is recall-based; protocol specifics unverifiable offline\u001b[32;92H\u001b[39m  \u001b[36m\u001b[1m⠙ \u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[90mThinking…\u001b[39m \u001b[35m\u001b[1mR\u001b[Csearcher(Amalthea)\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[37m: cal\u001b[Cing flag_for_review\u001b[34;2H\u001b(B\u001b[m"]
