[0.005, "o", "\u001b[4;1H\u001b[93m\u001b[1m→ RC4 decrypt with a single hardcoded ASCII key\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m (key ~8–32 bytes, stored in cleartext in the binary). Look for one heavily cross-referenced routine\r\nwith a classic RC4 KSA/PRGA (256-iter S-box loop with swaps) fed by a standard-alphabet base64 decoder. The same key likely decrypts C2 traffic\r\n(rel\u001b[Cvant to #q-crypto-keys). The decrypted set should include the C2 URL and build ID — Stealc's embedded config is minimal; target lists come from\r\nthe server.\u001b[39m                                                                                                                                          \r\n                                                                                                                                                   \r\n\u001b[93m💬 reply #q-embedded-pe\u001b[36m\u001b[1m Researcher(Amalthea)\u001b(B\u001b[m                                                                                                   \r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "\u001b[93m[ext\u001b[Crnal\u001b[Cknowledge] Working hypoth\u001b[Csis (full checks in !62 under #q-family-attr\u001b[Cbution): in the Stealc\u001b[1m v2\u001b[C\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93mrewr\u001b[Cte, the e\u001b[Cbedded PE is mo\u001b[Ct plausibly\r\na\u001b[1m Chrome App-Bound Encryption (ABE) bypass helper\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m injected into/run in Chrome's context to recover app-bound cookies/keys (v1 had no embedded PE — it\r\ndownloaded helper DLLs from C2). Verify via its strings (app_bound, os_crypt, encrypted_key, elevation, IElevator CLSID\r\n{708860E0-F641-4611-8895-7D867DD3675B}, named pipes) and the host-side consumer (injection APIs gated on a Chrome process). Alternative to rule out:\r\na generic\u001b[Cdr\u001b[Cpp\u001b[2C next-stage\u001b[Cpayload written to disk\u001b[Ca\u001b[Cd execut\u001b[Cd.\u001b[39m                                                                                   \r\n                                                                                                                                            "]
[0.000, "o", "         \r\n\u001b[93m💬 reply #q-ransomware\u001b[36m\u001b[1m Researcher(Amalthea)\u001b(B\u001b[m                                                                            \r\n\u001b[93m[external knowledge] Prior from family attribut\u001b[Con (#q-family-at\u001b[Cribution, YARA-matched Stealc): S\u001b[2Calc h\u001b[Cs\u001b[1m no ransomware capability\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m in\u001b[Cany public\u001b[39m  \r\n\u001b[93mv1/v2 \u001b[Ceporting —\u001b[2Co file encryption\u001b[Cof us\u001b[Cr\u001b[Cdata, no ransom notes, no shadow-copy deletion. Its crypto use is RC4 for string/traffic obfuscation and\r\nDPAPI/AES-GCM handling for\u001b[3m decrypting stolen browser credentials\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m, which can superficially trip ransomware heuristics. Falsifiable refutation checks:\r\nabsence of vssadmin/wmic shadowcopy/bcdedit strings, no ransom-note literals, no mass file-enumeration + rewrite-in-place loop; file enumeration\r\npres\u001b[Cnt should feed the\u001b[3m grabber/exfil\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m p\u001b[2Ch (read +\u001b[Cupload), no\u001b(B\u001b[m"]
[0.000, "o", "\u001b[93mt encrypt-and-overwrite.\u001b[39m                                                            \r\n                                                                                                                                                     \r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[39m \u001b[90mResearcher(Amalthea) · ▸ flag_for_review(researcher) · 930 chars\u001b[39m                                                                                  \r\n                                                                                                                                                \r\n\u001b[97m🤖\u001b[36m\u001b[1m researcher\u001b(B\u001b[m                                                                         \r\n\u001b[97m⚑ flagged for analyst review: Stealc v2 checklist is recall-based; protocol specifics unverifiable offline\r\n\u001b[39m \u001b[C                                                                \r\n\u001b[97m🤖\u001b[36m\u001b[1m ReverseEngineer(C\u001b(B\u001b[m"]
[0.000, "o", "\u001b[36m\u001b[1mallisto)\r\n\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93minit_decrypted_string_table\u001b[97m is one 20 KB function that decrypts everything. Best route: emulate it and capture the recovered strings and their\r\ndestination addresses.\u001b[39m                                                                                    \u001b[42;98H\u001b[90m3\u001b[C055\u001b[C230\u001b[9C4\u001b[C078\u001b[12C119\u001b[C308\u001b[6C28\u001b[C5\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[34;2H"]
[0.005, "o", "\u001b[4;1H\u001b[93m(relevant to #q-crypto-keys). The decrypted set\u001b[Cshould include the C2 URL and build ID —\u001b[CSt\u001b[Calc's embedded\u001b[Cc\u001b[Cnfig is minimal; targ\u001b[Ct lists come from\r\nthe server.\u001b[39m                                                                                                                                    \r\n                                                                                                                                                    \r\n\u001b[93m💬 reply #q-embedded-pe\u001b[36m\u001b[1m Researcher(Amalthea)\r\n\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m[external knowledge] Working hypothesis (full checks in !62 under #q-family-attribution): in the Stealc\u001b[1m v2\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m rewrite, the embedded PE is most plausibly\r\na\u001b[1m Chrome App-Bound Encryption (ABE) bypass helper\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m injected into/run in Chrome's context to recover app-bound cookies/keys (v1 had no embedded PE — it\r\ndownloaded help\u001b[Cr DLLs from C2). Verify\u001b[Cvia its s\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93mtrings\u001b[C(app_bound, os_crypt, encrypted_key,\u001b[Cel\u001b[Cva\u001b[Cion, IElevator CLSID\u001b[39m                              \r\n\u001b[93m{708860E0-F641-4611-8895-7D867DD3675B}, named pipes) and the h\u001b[Cst-side\u001b[Cconsumer\u001b[C(i\u001b[Cj\u001b[Cc\u001b[Cion\u001b[CAPIs gated on a Chr\u001b[Cme proc\u001b[Cs\u001b[C). Alternative to rule out:\u001b[39m \r\n\u001b[93ma generic drop\u001b[2Cd\u001b[Cnext-stage payload written \u001b[Co\u001b[Cdisk and executed.\u001b[39m                                                     \r\n                                                                                                                                                    \r\n\u001b[93m💬 reply #q-ransomware\u001b[36m\u001b[1m Researcher(Amalthea)\u001b(B\u001b[m                       \r\n\u001b[93m[external knowledge] Prior from family attribution (#q-family-attribution, YARA-matched Stealc): Stealc has\u001b[1m no ransomware capability\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m in any public\r\nv1\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m/v2 reporting — no file encryption of user data, no ransom notes, no shadow-copy deletion. Its crypto use is RC4 for string/traffic obfuscation and\r\nDPAPI/AES-GCM handling f\u001b[2C\u001b[3m decrypting stolen browser credentials\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m, which can superfi\u001b[Cially trip ransomware heuristics. Falsifiable refutation checks:\r\nabsence of vssadmi\u001b[C/wm\u001b[Cc shadowcopy/bcdedit strings, no ransom-note\u001b[Cliter\u001b[Cls, n\u001b[C mass file-enumeration + r\u001b[Cwrite-in-place loop; file enumeration\u001b[39m     \r\n\u001b[93mpresent should feed the\u001b[3m grabbe\u001b[C/exfil\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m path (read + upload), not encrypt-and-overwr\u001b[Cte.\u001b[39m                                                              \r\n                                                                                                                                                \r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[39m \u001b[90mResearcher(Amalthea) · ▸ flag_for_review(researcher) · 930 chars\u001b[39m                    \u001b[23;1H\u001b[97m🤖\u001b[36m\u001b[1m researcher\u001b(B\u001b[m                                                     \r\n\u001b[97m⚑ flagged for analyst review: Stealc v2 checklist is recall-based; protocol specifics unverifiable offline\r\n\u001b[39m \u001b[C           \r\n\u001b[97m🤖\u001b[36m\u001b[1m ReverseEngineer(Callisto)\u001b(B\u001b[m                                                                              \r\n\u001b[93minit_decrypted_string_table\u001b[97m is one 20 KB function that decrypts everything. Best route: emulate it and capture the recovered strings and their\r\ndestination addresses.\u001b[39m      \r\n                                                                                                                                              \r\n\u001b[90m○\u001b[39m \u001b[90mReverseEngineer(Callisto) · ▸ emulate_function(init_decrypted_string_table) · …running\u001b[32;88H\u001b[36m\u001b[1m⠇ \u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[90mThinking…\u001b[39m \u001b[35m\u001b[1mReverseEngineer(Callisto)\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[37m: cal\u001b[Cing emulate_funct\u001b[Con\u001b[34;2H\u001b(B\u001b[m"]
