[0.008, "o", "\u001b[4;1H\u001b[32m●\u001b[C\u001b[90mReverseEngineer(Ananke) · ▸ xrefs_to(0x140072180) · 2 chars\r\n\u001b[39m \u001b[C                                                                              \r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[C\u001b[90mReverseEngineer(Ananke) · ▸ xrefs_to(0x140072280) · 2 chars\r\n\u001b[39m \u001b[C                                                                      \r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[C\u001b[90mReverseEngineer(Ananke) · ▸ read(0x140072100) · 256 chars\r\n\u001b[39m \u001b[C                                                           \r\n\u001b[97m🤖\u001b[36m\u001b[1m Developer(Metis)\r\n\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[97mBoth robustness checks done. The 6 real short tokens include a date tuple (\"11\",\"04\",\"20\",\"25\" — likely the Stealc v2 date kill-switch, relevant to\r\n#q-anti-analysis). Now let me also sweep the\u001b[1m whole raw file\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[97m (both encodings) for the ransomware keywords, so the absence claim isn't limited to the\r\ndecrypted table:\u001b[39m                                           \u001b[15;1H\u001b[32m●\u001b[C\u001b[90mDeveloper(Metis) · ▸ grep_bytes([\"vssadmin\",\"shadowcopy\",\"shadow copy\",…) · 281 chars\r\n\u001b[39m                                                                                                                                                   \r\n\u001b[97m🤖\u001b[36m\u001b[1m ReverseEngineer(Ananke)\u001b(B\u001b[m                                                                                                                         \r\n\u001b[97mThe shuffle tables span a large region with no direct Ghidra xrefs at the bases I probed. Let me delegate a scan for RIP-relative references into the\r\nwhole region.\r\n\u001b[39m \u001b[C                                                                                     \r\n\u001b[90m○\u001b[CReverseEngineer(Ananke) · ▸ developer(Prove the detector-flagged .rdata addre…) · …running\r\n\u001b[39m \u001b[C                      