[0.011, "o", "\u001b[4;1H                                            \r\n\u001b[97m🤖\u001b[36m\u001b[1m Developer(Leda)\u001b(B\u001b[m                                \r\n\u001b[97mBoth verification routes agree on every spot-check. Now I'll post the complete list under #q-bulk-strings as grouped evidence comments.\r\n\u001b[39m                              \r\n\u001b[93m💬 reply #q-bulk-strings\u001b[36m\u001b[1m Developer(Leda)\r\n\u001b[93mMethodology — bulk decrypt complete (session script \u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93mbulk_decrypt_b64_rc4\u001b[1m).\r\n\u001b(B\u001b[m \u001b[C                 \r\n\u001b[93mArgs: {base:\"14006f000\", size:107008, key:\"CgeOvEO7hu\", min_len:8}.\u001b[39m                                                                              \r\n                                                                                                                                                   \r\n\u001b[93mMethod: read ALL of .rdata (0x14006f000–0x1400891ff, 107,008 bytes) from program memory; \u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93mscan for NUL-bounded runs of base64-charset bytes with\r\nlength ≥ 8 (proper C strings only: preceded and followed by NUL) →\u001b[1m 337 candidate tokens\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m; decode each with the proven pipeline base64 →\r\nRC4(\"CgeOvEO7hu\") (native decode_bytes binding); keep results that are 100% printable ASCII →\u001b[1m 270 plaintexts recovered\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m. The 67 rejected runs are\r\nordinary plain-ASCII .rdata strings (CRT literals, import names) whose pseudo-decode is garbage — correct rejections.\r\n\u001b[39m \u001b[C                                                                   \r\n\u001b[93mIndependent re-verification (second route = raw file bytes + native decode_bytes tool, vs. script's Ghidra-memory route):\r\n\u001b[39m \u001b[C                    \r\n\u001b[93m- /x6mNTpQ8CGGjZQ= @ 0x14007d838 → 45.93.20.28 (both routes)\u001b[39m                                                                                  \r\n\u001b[93m- iETmeGwQtjz8... @ 0x14007e4a0 (file 0x7d6a0) → Content-T\u001b(B\u001b[m"]
[0.000, "o", "\u001b[93mype: application/json\\r\\n —\u001b[1m note: the trailing\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m \\r\\n\u001b[1m is four literal characters\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m 5c 72 5c 6e\r\n\u001b[1m(backslash-r-backslash-n), not CRLF\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m (hexdump-verified)\r\n- jE78T3wM... @ 0x14007e060 (file 0x7d260) → GetCurrentProcessId —\u001b[1m corrects !73's \"GetCerrentProcessId (sic)\"\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m: the binary holds token\r\njE78T**3**wM...; the mistyped token jE78T2wM occurs nowhere (search_memory = 0 hits). The typo was in !73's token transcription, not in the malware.\r\n\u001b[39m \u001b[C                                                       \r\n\u001b[93mCategory totals (script's heuristic grouping, full lists in the following comments): C2/network 15, registry 3, browser DB files 3, paths/masks 24,\r\napp targets 6, API names 122, report templates 30, other (JSON protocol/config tokens etc.) 67 =\u001b[1m 270\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m.\u001b[29;1HCoverage statement: whole .rdata section scanned; tokens shorter than 8 base64 chars (plaintexts ≤ ~5 bytes) excluded per task spec; .data not\r\nscanned (the str\u001b(B\u001b[m"]
