[0.000, "o", "                                         \r\n\u001b[97m🤖\u001b[36m\u001b[1m ReverseEngineer(Carme)\r\n\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[97mNow posting the evidence comments under #q-ransomware.\u001b[39m                                            \u001b[16;1H\u001b[93m💬 reply #q-ransomware\u001b[36m\u001b[1m ReverseEngineer(Carme)\u001b(B\u001b[m                \r\n\u001b[93m\u001b[1mDetector-site triage: every T1486 anchor site is a false positive (code-level evidence).\r\n\u001b(B\u001b[m \u001b[C                                                                          \r\n\u001b[94m1.\u001b[93m FindNextFile @ 0x14005c1f7 sits inside FUN_14005c018 (renamed crt_expand_argv_wildcards). Decompile shows it is the\u001b[1m MSVC CRT argv\r\nwildcard-expansion routine\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m: scans the command line for */? via wcspbrk(_Str, L\"*?\"), calls FindFirstFileExW/FindNextFileW only to expand command-line\r\nglobs, and hands the result to __acrt_allocate_buffer_for_argv (a named CRT symbol in the same function). It has\u001b(B\u001b[m"]
[0.000, "o", "\u001b[93m\u001b[1m zero callers in malware code\u001b(B\u001b[m     \r\n\u001b[93m(invoked only from CRT startup). It never opens,\u001b[Creads, or writes file contents.\u001b[39m                                                                     \r\n\u001b[94m2.\u001b[93m FUN_140001e10 (#fn-140001e10) is a 3-line C++ bad_array_new_length\u001b[1m exception-object constructor\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m (param_1[1] = \"bad array new length\"; *param_1 =\r\n&PTR_FUN_140073218;). The three T1486 \"recovered strings\" there (!5–!7) are XOR replays of the junk key \"address in use\" (a CRT winsock error text\r\nfragment) over other CRT error text — the \"plaintexts\" (efwasmk!a{, t{s\"emd<rz7, eoilqx<Ll+hyead`) are\u001b[1m gibberish, not language\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m: they are false\r\ndecodes, not recovered keys or payloads.\r\n\u001b[94m3.\u001b[93m FUN_140002b6c (#fn-140002b6c) is the pre-main\u001b[1m string-table initializer\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m (proven in !60); its 18 \"candidate symmetric keys\" (!8–!25) are\u001b[1m ciphertext\r\nblobs\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m of the base64+RC4(\"CgeOv\u001b(B\u001b[m"]
[0.000, "o", "\u001b[93mEO7hu\") string table, already individually decrypted to API names/paths in !61/!73.\r\n\u001b[39m \u001b[C                       \r\n\u001b[93mSo the detector's \"crypto material + file enumeration\" premise rested on CRT startup code, CRT error strings, and string-obfuscation ciphertext.\u001b[32;93H\u001b[36m\u001b[1m⠹ \u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[90mThinking…\u001b[C\u001b[35m\u001b[1mReverseEngineer(Carm\u001b[C)\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[37m: calling cre\u001b[Cte_comm\u001b[Cnt\u001b(B\u001b[m"]
