[0.020, "o", "\u001b[5;1H\u001b[97m🤖\u001b[36m\u001b[1m ReverseEngineer(Carme)\u001b(B\u001b[m                            \r\n\u001b[97mNow posting the evidence comments under #q-ransomware.\r\n\u001b[39m \u001b[C                       \r\n\u001b[93m💬 reply #q-ransomware\u001b[36m\u001b[1m ReverseEngineer(Carme)\u001b(B\u001b[m         \r\n\u001b[93m\u001b[1mDetector-site triage: every T1486 anchor site is a false positive (code-level evidence).\r\n\u001b(B\u001b[m \u001b[C                                           \r\n\u001b[94m1.\u001b[93m FindNextFile @ 0x14005c1f7 sits inside FUN_14005c018 (renamed crt_expand_argv_wildcards). Decompile shows it is the\u001b[1m MSVC CRT argv\r\nwildcard-expansion routine\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m: scans the command line for */? via wcspbrk(_Str, L\"*?\"), calls FindFirstFileExW/FindNextFileW only to expand command-line\r\nglobs, and hands the result to __acrt_allocate_buffer_for_argv (a nam\u001b[Cd CRT symbol in the same function). It\u001b[Chas\u001b[1m zero callers in malware code\r\n\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m(invoked only from CRT startup). I\u001b[C never opens, reads, or\u001b[Cwrites file contents.\u001b[39m                                                                     \r\n\u001b[94m2.\u001b[93m FUN_140001e10\u001b[C(#fn-140001e10) is a 3-\u001b[Cine C++ bad_array_new_length\u001b[1m exception-object constructor\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m (param_1[1] = \"bad array new length\"; *param_1 =\r\n&PTR_FUN_140073218;). The three T1486 \"rec\u001b[Cv\u001b[Cred\u001b[Cstrings\" there (!5–!7) are XOR replays of the junk key \"address in use\" (a CRT winsock error text\r\nfragment) over other CRT error text\u001b[C—\u001b[Cthe \"plaintexts\" (efwasmk!a{, t{s\"emd<rz7, eoilqx<Ll+hyead`)\u001b[Care\u001b[1m gibberish, not language\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m:\u001b[Cthey are\u001b[Cfalse\u001b[39m     \r\n\u001b[93mdecodes, not recovered k\u001b[Cys or payloads.\u001b[39m                                                                                                          \r\n\u001b[94m3.\u001b[93m FUN_140002b6c (#fn-140002b6c) is\u001b[Cthe\u001b(B\u001b[m"]
[0.000, "o", "\u001b[93m pre-m\u001b[3C\u001b[1m string-table initializer\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m (p\u001b[Coven in !60); its 18 \"candidate symmetric keys\" (!8–!25) are\u001b[1m ciphertext\r\nblobs\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m of\u001b[Cthe\u001b[Cbase64+RC4(\"CgeOvEO7hu\") \u001b[Ctring table, already individually decrypted to API names/paths in !61/!73.\r\n\u001b[39m                                                                                                                                                    \r\n\u001b[93mSo the de\u001b[Cector's \"crypto material + file enumeration\" premise rested on\u001b[CCRT startup code, CRT error strings, and string-obfuscation ciphertext.\u001b[24;1H\u001b[97m🤖\u001b[36m\u001b[1m Developer(Themisto)\u001b(B\u001b[m                                                                                                                          \r\n\u001b[97mThe lone\u001b[93m pshufb\u001b[97m at 0x14003e730 is a broadcast (\u001b[93mpshufb xmm1, xmm0\u001b[97m with zeroed mask) inside a memchr-style SSE routine — it does\u001b[1m not\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[97m touch Range A.\r\nCheck for VEX-encoded\u001b[93m vpshufb\u001b[97m (C4 E2 xx 00) and other 0F38-map AVX2 consumers:\r\n\u001b[39m                                                                                                                                                 \r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[39m \u001b[90mDeveloper(Themisto) · ▸ search_memory(c4e2) · 562 chars\u001b[39m                     \u001b[30;1H\u001b[90m○\u001b[25Cr\u001b[2Cd(14003e690) · …running\u001b[39m     \u001b[32;97H         \u001b[36m\u001b[1m⠦ \u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[90mThinking…\u001b[39m \u001b[35m\u001b[1mDev\u001b[Cloper(Themisto)\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[37m: c\u001b[Clling read\u001b(B\u001b[m"]
