[0.045, "o", "\u001b[4;1H\u001b[93m\u001b[1mVerdict: ransomware (T1486) REFUTED — this is the Stealc v2 infostealer.\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m The file enumeration feeds (a) the server-configured file grabber and\u001b[Cthe\u001b[39m \r\n\u001b[93mhardcoded Steam-credential\u001b[Crule (r\u001b[Cad → in-memory collection → RC4+base64 JSON exfil to 45.93.20.28/3d15e67552d448ff.php) and (b) browser-profile\r\nharve\u001b[Cting via temporary staging copies; the detect\u001b[2C's T1486 anchors are CRT code and string-obfusc\u001b[Ction ciphertext (!125). Calibration: this\u001b[39m       \r\n\u001b[93mexcludes embedded encryption\u001b[Ccapability (no write-back loop, no\u001b[Crename, no\u001b[Cshadow-copy\u001b[Cdel\u001b[Ction, no ransom strings — each exclusion cited above);\r\nwhat it does\u001b[1m not\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m exclude is the C2-tasked loader (download_url_to_file + IEX cradle + msiexec) delivering an arbitrary follow-on payload — downloader\r\ncapability by design, not ransomware capability in this binary.\u001b[39m                                                                                   \r\n                                                                                                                                                 \r\n\u001b[31m\u001b[1m⚠ ReverseEngineer(Carme) context warning (flush findings to the forum): ~160733 of 200000 tokens\u001b(B\u001b[m                                              \r\n                                                                                                                                                 \r\n\u001b[93m💬 reply #fn-140001e10\u001b[36m\u001b[1m ReverseEngineer(Carme)\u001b(B\u001b[m                                                                                                        \r\n\u001b[93mAll three T1486 f\u001b[Cndings here are false positives:\u001b[CFUN_140001e10 is a 3-line C++ bad_array_new_length exception-object constructor (CRT), and the\r\n\"recovered strings\" (!5–!7) are XOR replays of the junk key \"address in use\" over CRT error tex\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93mt producing gibberish, not language. Full triage with\r\ndecompile evidence: !125 under #q-ransomware (where T1486 is refuted end-to-end).\u001b[39m               \u001b[18;1H\u001b[32m●\u001b[C\u001b[90mReverseEngineer(Carme) · ▸ set_comment(0x1400082d8) · 99 chars\r\n\u001b[39m                                                                                                                                                 \r\n\u001b[97m🤖\u001b[36m\u001b[1m ReverseEngineer(Pasiphae)\u001b(B\u001b[m                                                                                                                        \r\n\u001b[93mDAT_1400b4\u001b[C20\u001b[97m name slot = 0x1400b08d0 = \u001b[1mShellExecuteExA\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[97m (byte-traced + table). Now verifying the name feeding \u001b[93mDAT_1400b4e08\u001b[97m (slot 0x1400b1d90).\u001b[23;19H\u001b[90mP\u001b[Csiphae) · ▸ xrefs_to\u001b[7Cb1d90\u001b[4C663 chars\u001b[25;1H\u001b[32m●\u001b[C\u001b[90mReverseEngineer(Pasiphae) · ▸ read(0x140002\u001b(B\u001b[m"]
[0.000, "o", "\u001b[90m558) · 128 chars\r\n\u001b[39m                                                                                                                                               \r\n\u001b[97m🤖\u001b[36m\u001b[1m Developer(Themisto)\r\n\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[97mNo memory-form\u001b[93m vpshufb\u001b[97m either — the AVX2 code uses tail-mask tables at 0x140073190+ (\u001b[93mvpmaskmov\u001b[97m), and the 0x140064xxx hits are FMA scalar math;\r\n0x140064ee2 is a\u001b[93m call rel32\u001b[97m false positive. So Range A/B are reached (if at all) via register-relative constant displacements, which only the\r\ndecompiler will fold into absolute addresses. Plan: decompile all functions in batches and grep the pseudocode for addresses in the two ranges:\u001b[32;100H\u001b[36m\u001b[1m⠴\u001b[42;98H\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[90m357\u001b[C878\u001b[10C9\u001b[2C08\u001b[13C847\u001b[C386\u001b[8C39\u001b(B\u001b[m"]
