[0.060, "o", "\u001b[4;1H                                                                                                                                                    \r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[39m \u001b[90mReverseEngineer(Ananke) · ▸ complete_task(reverse-engineer) · 314 chars\u001b[39m                                                                          \r\n                                             \r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[C\u001b[90mReverseEngineer(Ananke) · ▸ complete_task(reverse-engineer) · 239 chars\r\n\u001b[39m \u001b[C                                                                       \r\n\u001b[93m💬 reply #fn-140001e10\u001b[36m\u001b[1m ReverseEngineer(Ananke)\r\n\u001b[93mDisproof of the T1486 findings (!5, !6, !7):\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m FUN_140001e10 is the std::bad_array_new_length exception constructor — its full decompile is three\r\nassignments (param_1[1] = \"bad array new length\"; *param_1 = &vftable). It performs no cryptography.\r\n\u001b[39m \u001b[C                                            \r\n\u001b[93mThe \"key\" \"address in use\" is the MSVC STL std::generic_category message string at 0x1400705b8 (ne\u001b[Cghbors: s\u001b[2Ccess,\u001b[Caddress family not\u001b[Csuppo\u001b[Ct\u001b[Cd,\u001b[14;2Hddress \u001b[Cot\u001b[Cav\u001b[Cilable, already\u001b[Cconnect\u001b[Cd, ar\u001b[Cument list too long — raw read at 0x140070560). The \"ciphertext\" at 0x140075071/0x14007507d/0x14007509d\r\nis the interior of the UCRT strtod large_power_indices {u16,u8,u8} record table (base 0x140075010), followed at 0x1400750a8 by the powers-of-ten u32\r\ntable 10…10^9, consumed only by UCRT strtod (FUN_1400462f4) and th\u001b[C float formatter (FUN_14006081c). Replaying the XOR over\u001b[Cr\u001b[Cw file bytes yiel\u001b[Cs\r\ngarbage\u001b[C(acwwd{8hf5X…`). All three findings are coincidental decodes of legitimate CRT constant data — no Data-Encrypted-for-Impact behavior exists\u001b[39m \r\n\u001b[93mhere. Full evidence: !136, !137 under #q-decoded-config.\u001b(B\u001b[m"]
[0.001, "o", "                                                                                            \r\n                                                                                                                                                 \r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[39m \u001b[90mReverseEngineer(Ananke) · ▸ set_status(#q-decoded-config) · 7290 chars\u001b[39m                                                                           \r\n                                                        \r\n\u001b[93m💬 reply #q-ransomware\u001b[36m\u001b[1m ReverseEngineer(Ananke)\r\n\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93mSupporting the refutation: the strongest detector \"encryption\" evidence — the T1486 findings on FUN_140001e10 (#fn-140001e10 !5/!6/!7, XOR key\r\n\"address in use\") — has been disproved by raw-byte replay and consumer tracing (see !136/!137 under #q-decoded-config and !138). The function is the\r\nstd::bad_array_new_length exception constructor; the \"key\" is an STL errc message string; the \"ciphertext\" is the UCRT strtod power-of-ten index\r\ntable co\u001b[Csumed\u001b[Conly by strtod/float-formatting. Lik\u001b[Cwise \u001b[Che ADD-key \"shell command\" decode\u001b[2Ci\u001b[2C#fn-\u001b[4C3e62c\u001b[Care a CRT SIMD\u001b[C16−2·popcount(i) byte\r\nLUT shifted\u001b[Cinto printabl\u001b[C range. None\u001b[Cof\u001b[Cthese contribute any e\u001b[Ccryption-for-impact capability.\u001b[39m                                                    \r\n                                                                                                                                                \r\n\u001b[97m🤖\u001b[36m\u001b[1m ReverseEngineer(Ananke)\u001b(B\u001b[m                                                                                                                        \r\n\u001b[97mThe question is solved. Let me persist the identifications as Ghidra comments and rename the key runtime functions so future runs see them.\u001b[42;98H\u001b[90m368\u001b[C568\u001b[10C4\u001b[C022\u001b[13C912\u001b[C590\u001b[8C58\u001b[34;2H\u001b(B\u001b[m"]
[0.007, "o", "\u001b[5;65H\u001b[90m239\u001b[7;1H\u001b[93m💬 reply #fn-140001e10\u001b[36m\u001b[1m ReverseEngineer(Ananke)\u001b(B\u001b[m                           \r\n\u001b[93m\u001b[1mDisproof of the T1486 findings (!5, !6, !7):\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m FUN_140001e10 is the std::bad_array_new_length exception constructor — its full decompile is three\r\nassignments (param_1[1] = \"bad array new length\"; *param_1 = &vftable). It performs no cryptography.\r\n\u001b[39m                                                                                                                                               \r\n\u001b[93mThe \"key\" \"address in use\" is the MSVC STL std::generic_category message string at 0x1400705b8 (neighbors: success, address family not supported,\r\naddress not available, already connected, argument list too long — raw read at 0x140070560). The \"ciphertext\" at 0x140075071/0x14007507d/0x14007509d\r\nis the interio\u001b[C of\u001b[Cthe UCRT \u001b[Ctrtod large_power_indices {u16,u8,u8} record \u001b[Cable\u001b[C(base 0x14\u001b[C075010), followed at 0x1400750a8\u001b[Cby the\u001b[Cp\u001b[Cwers-of-\u001b[2Cn u32\r\ntabl\u001b[C 10…10^9, consumed only by UCRT strtod (FUN_1400462f4)\u001b[Cand the float formatter (FUN_14006081c). R\u001b[Cplaying \u001b[Che XOR over raw file bytes yields\u001b[39m   \r\n\u001b[93mgarbage (acwwd{8h\u001b[C5X…`). All \u001b[Chree\u001b[Cfindings a\u001b[Ce co\u001b[Cncidental decodes of legitimate CRT constant data — n\u001b[C Dat\u001b[C-Encrypted-for-Impact behavi\u001b[Cr exists\u001b[39m \r\n\u001b[93mhere.\u001b[CFull evidence: !136, !137\u001b[Cunder #q-decoded-config.\u001b[39m                                                                                         \r\n                                                                                                                                                   \r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[39m \u001b[90mReverseEngineer(Ananke) · ▸ set_status(#q-decoded-config) · 7290 chars\u001b[20;1H\u001b[93m💬 reply #q-ransomware\u001b[36m\u001b[1m ReverseEngineer(Ananke)\u001b(B\u001b[m                          \r\n\u001b[93mSupporting the refutation: the strongest detector \"encryption\" evidence — the T1486 findings on FUN_140001e10 (#fn-140001e10 !5/!6/!7, XOR key\r\n\"address\u001b[Cin use\") — has been disproved by raw-byte replay and consumer tracing (see !136/!137 under #q-decoded-config and !138). The function is the\r\nstd::bad_array_n\u001b[Cw_length exc\u001b[Cption constructor; th\u001b[C \"key\" is an STL errc\u001b[Cmessage str\u001b[2Cg; the \"ciphertext\" is\u001b[Cthe UCRT strtod power-of-ten\u001b[Cindex\r\nt\u001b[Cble consumed only\u001b[Cby strtod/float-formatting. Likewise the ADD-key \"shell command\" decodes in #fn-14003e62c are a CRT SIMD 16−2·popcoun\u001b[C(i)\u001b[Cbyte\u001b[39m  \r\n\u001b[93mLUT shifted into printable range. None of these contribute\u001b[Cany encryption-for-impac\u001b[C capability.\u001b[39m                                                \r\n                                                                                                                                                  \r\n\u001b[97m🤖\u001b[36m\u001b[1m ReverseEngineer(Ananke)\u001b(B\u001b[m                                                                      \r\n\u001b[97mThe question is solved. Let me persist the identifications as Ghidra comments and rename the key runtime functions so future runs see them.\r\n\u001b[39m \u001b[C                        \r\n\u001b[90m○\u001b[39m \u001b[90mReverseEngineer(Ananke) · ▸ set_comment(0x140072580) · …running\u001b[39m                                                                          \u001b[32;92H   \u001b[36m\u001b[1m⠼ \u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[90mTh\u001b[2Cking…\u001b[39m \u001b[35m\u001b[1mRev\u001b[CrseE\u001b[Cgineer(Ananke)\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[37m: calling set\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[34;2H"]
[0.007, "o", "\u001b[5;1H\u001b[93m💬 reply #fn-140001e10\u001b[36m\u001b[1m ReverseEngineer(Ananke)\u001b(B\u001b[m                           \r\n\u001b[93m\u001b[1mDisproof of the T1486 findings (!5, !6, !7):\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[93m FUN_140001e10 is the std::bad_array_new_length exception constructor — its full decompile is three\r\nassignments (param_1[1] = \"bad array new length\"; *param_1 = &vftable). It performs no cryptography.\r\n\u001b[39m                                                                                                                                               \r\n\u001b[93mThe \"key\" \"address in use\" is the MSVC STL std::generic_category message string at 0x1400705b8 (neighbors: success, address family not supported,\r\naddress not available, already connected, argument list too long — raw read at 0x140070560). The \"ciphertext\" at 0x140075071/0x14007507d/0x14007509d\r\nis the interio\u001b[C of\u001b[Cthe UCRT \u001b[Ctrtod large_power_indices {u16,u8,u8} record \u001b[Cable\u001b[C(base 0x14\u001b[C075010), followed at 0x1400750a8\u001b[Cby the\u001b[Cp\u001b[Cwers-of-\u001b[2Cn u32\r\ntabl\u001b[C 10…10^9, consumed only by UCRT strtod (FUN_1400462f4)\u001b[Cand the float formatter (FUN_14006081c). R\u001b[Cplaying \u001b[Che XOR over raw file bytes yields\u001b[39m   \r\n\u001b[93mgarbage (acwwd{8h\u001b[C5X…`). All \u001b[Chree\u001b[Cfindings a\u001b[Ce co\u001b[Cncidental decodes of legitimate CRT constant data — n\u001b[C Dat\u001b[C-Encrypted-for-Impact behavi\u001b[Cr exists\u001b[39m \r\n\u001b[93mhere.\u001b[CFull evidence: !136, !137\u001b[Cunder #q-decoded-config.\u001b[39m                                                                                         \r\n                                                                                                                                                   \r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[39m \u001b[90mReverseEngineer(Ananke) · ▸ set_status(#q-decoded-config) · 7290 chars\u001b[18;1H\u001b[93m💬 reply #q-ransomware\u001b[36m\u001b[1m ReverseEngineer(Ananke)\u001b(B\u001b[m                          \r\n\u001b[93mSupporting the refutation: the strongest detector \"encryption\" evidence — the T1486 findings on FUN_140001e10 (#fn-140001e10 !5/!6/!7, XOR key\r\n\"address\u001b[Cin use\") — has been disproved by raw-byte replay and consumer tracing (see !136/!137 under #q-decoded-config and !138). The function is the\r\nstd::bad_array_n\u001b[Cw_length exc\u001b[Cption constructor; th\u001b[C \"key\" is an STL errc\u001b[Cmessage str\u001b[2Cg; the \"ciphertext\" is\u001b[Cthe UCRT strtod power-of-ten\u001b[Cindex\r\nt\u001b[Cble consumed only\u001b[Cby strtod/float-formatting. Likewise the ADD-key \"shell command\" decodes in #fn-14003e62c are a CRT SIMD 16−2·popcoun\u001b[C(i)\u001b[Cbyte\u001b[39m  \r\n\u001b[93mLUT shifted into printable range. None of these contribute\u001b[Cany encryption-for-impac\u001b[C capability.\u001b[39m                                                \r\n                                                                                                                                                  \r\n\u001b[97m🤖\u001b[36m\u001b[1m ReverseEngineer(Ananke)\u001b(B\u001b[m                                                                      \r\n\u001b[97mThe question is solved. Let me persist the identifications as Ghidra comments and rename the key runtime functions so future runs see them.\r\n\u001b[39m \u001b[C                        \r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[39m \u001b[90mReverseEngineer(Ananke) · ▸ set_comment(0x140072580) · 99 chars\u001b[39m                                                                          \u001b[30;50H\u001b[90m501\u001b[34;2H\u001b(B\u001b[m"]
