[0.008, "o", "\u001b[4;1H                                                                                                                                                     \r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[39m \u001b[90mDeveloper(Harpalyke) · ▸ follow_link(#q-residual-decodes) · 767 chars\u001b[7;28Hlist_programs() · 211 chars\u001b[39m                 \u001b[9;28H\u001b[90mrhai\u001b[Cdocs() · 634\u001b[Clines\u001b[39m    \u001b[11;1H\u001b[97m🤖\u001b[36m\u001b[1m Developer(Harpalyke)\u001b(B\u001b[m                           \r\n\u001b[97mLet me test how the script disassembly binding behaves with mid-function addresses.\r\n\u001b[39m \u001b[C                     \r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[39m \u001b[90mDeveloper(Harpalyke) · ▸ create_script(test_disasm_anchor) · 73 chars\u001b[39m            \u001b[16;28H\u001b[90mrun_script(tes\u001b[C_disasm_anchor) · 258 chars\u001b[39m  \u001b[18;1H\u001b[97m🤖\u001b[36m\u001b[1m Developer(Harpalyke)\u001b(B\u001b[m                                              \r\n\u001b[97mThe bindi\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[97mng starts disassembly at the given address. Now I'll build a script that walks from each function start in 100-instruction chunks\r\n(guaranteeing correct alignment), finds the containing instruction, and dumps the ±8 window with resolved data refs.\r\n\u001b[39m                                                                                                                                          \r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[39m \u001b[90mDeveloper(Harpalyke) · ▸ create_script(site_context) · 68 chars\u001b[39m                                                   \u001b[24;1H\u001b[90m○\u001b[26Crun_script(site_contex\u001b[C) · …running\u001b[39m   \r\n\u001b[90m\u001b[3m     14000ab22  CMP RAX, 0x1f\r\n  >> 14000ab26  JA 0x14000b1d1\u001b(B\u001b[m                                \u001b[27;3H\u001b[90m\u001b[3m   14000ab2c  CALL 0x14003f24c\u001b(B\u001b[m                 \u001b[28;5H\u001b[90m\u001b[3m  call -> \u001b[Cx14003f24c (thu\u001b[Ck